\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Coupled circuit simulation of the shared series dc-link current $i_{\mathrm  {dc}}(t)$ for $N=3$ SPB cells ($M=0.85$, $\cos \varphi \approx 0.94$, $f_1=1$~Hz, $f_{sw}=100$~Hz), without (red) and with (blue) carrier interleaving applied \emph  {between the three submodules} (each cell's own carrier phase-shifted, not the fixed $120^\circ $ a/b/c reference separation within a cell), using the same mechanism as \eqref  {eq:carrier_phase}. Each cell's own naturally-sampled SPWM switching function drives a local converter-side current $i_{\mathrm  {conv},k}(t)$; because the $N$ cells are connected in series, one common current $i_{\mathrm  {dc}}(t)$ flows through the shared bus/wire inductance from the battery, and each cell's local dc-link capacitor $C_k$ (Section~\ref {sec:operating_principle}) supplies exactly the difference $i_{\mathrm  {dc}}-i_{\mathrm  {conv},k}$, per $C_k\,dV_k/dt=i_{\mathrm  {dc}}-i_{\mathrm  {conv},k}$ and $L\,di_{\mathrm  {dc}}/dt=V_{\mathrm  {batt}}-Ri_{\mathrm  {dc}}-\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _kV_k$, solved self-consistently (shown after transients settle). Both traces share the same $\approx 1$~p.u.\ dc average (set by the converters' average power draw, independent of interleaving, consistent with \eqref  {eq:battery_current_tf}); interleaving reduces the peak-to-peak ripple by roughly $4$--$5\times $. The corresponding harmonic content is characterized analytically, not empirically, by the double-Fourier survival condition derived above (\eqref  {eq:dfs_switching}--\eqref  {eq:battery_current_tf}): only carrier harmonics that are themselves multiples of $N$ survive interleaving, each still carrying its own $n=\pm 6p-3$/$\pm 6p$ sideband family. This is a computed circuit-simulation result, not measured hardware data.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dclink_ripple_interleaving}{{9}{9}{}{figure.9}{}}
