\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Switching-function simulation of $N=2$ SPB common-mode cancellation ($M=0.85$, $\cos \varphi \approx 0.94$), all quantities in per unit. (a)~Each cell's own \emph  {absolute} common-mode voltage $v_{\mathrm  {cm},k}^{\mathrm  {abs}}=V_{-,k}+\big  [(S_a+S_b+S_c)/3-\tfrac  12\big  ]/N$, combining the stack offset $V_{-,k}$~\eqref  {eq:spb_cm_offset} (dashed line marks $V_{-,2}=V_{\mathrm  {dc}}/N=0.5$) with a six-step local switching term from the three independent phase-leg transitions; cell~2 uses the logically inverted gate commands of cell~1 plus a small inter-cell propagation delay $t_d=0.02\,T_{sw}$. (b)~Total common-mode voltage $v_{\mathrm  {cm,tot}}(t)$~\eqref  {eq:spb_cm_cancellation}: without cancellation the local terms add, swinging fully around the same stack-offset average; with cancellation the local terms cancel, leaving only narrow delay-induced residual spikes on top of that same average -- both settle to $\approx \!0.25=\text  {mean}(V_{-,1},V_{-,2})$, confirming that pairwise cancellation removes the switching ripple but not the structural stack offset itself. (c)~Resulting bearing/ground current through a capacitive coupling path, $i_{\mathrm  {bear}}=C\,dv_{\mathrm  {cm,tot}}/dt$, normalized to the without-cancellation peak: verified numerically that the constant stack offset in (b) contributes zero current (a constant has zero derivative) even though it dominates the voltage, so the current with cancellation is driven entirely by the delay-induced spikes. This is a computed simulation result, not measured hardware data.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spb_cm_cancellation}{{11}{11}{}{figure.11}{}}
